**Convention 5.1 (BRW hypotheses and their precise consumer).** Bluhm,
Rauber and Wolf, *Quantum Compression Relative to a Set of Measurements*,
[arXiv:1708.04898v4](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1708.04898v4), Definition 4.1
(PDF p. 6), consider CPTP encoding $M_D\to M_d\otimes\mathbb C^n$
and decoding back to $M_D$, with $n\in\mathbb N$. Their constraints
preserve specified measurement statistics for every input density matrix.
The allowed classical alphabet has arbitrary finite size. The model does
not include a pre-supplied input label, an accessible correlated archive or
an uncounted quantum bypass.

Lemma 5.2 (PDF pp. 7–8) preserves the composite channel using
$n_0\leq D^4$ classical blocks, starting from finite $n$. In its proof the
projected branch maps $T_i$ are CP, explicitly not necessarily TP. Their
Kraus operators factor through a $d$-dimensional space. This is exactly
the mature rank mechanism used in (QC.32)–(QC.33), without assigning TP
to an individual encoder branch or to a normalized postselected map.
The paper's §9.4, Definition 9.1 (PDF p. 27), also specifies state-family
compression by requiring all Hermitian expectations on each allowed state,
which is equality of that state's output density.

The original source supplies only the family (QC.19), not the all-density
input premise in Definition 4.1. The bridge is (QC.24)–(QC.30): actual
reachability, connected nonzero overlaps and a full three-dimensional span
force (QC.31). Only after this bridge is the composite the identity on all
$M_3$. BRW's all-input observable task can then be invoked on this live
space. Neither its Definition 4.1 nor Lemma 5.2 states an overlap-graph
dimension formula; the displayed source calculations are essential.

The number $D^4=81$ for $D=3$ bounds a possible classical factorization of
one composite channel in that lemma. It does not bound an original-control
observer's archive, failure-time precision, programme storage, or required
record labels. The lemma preserves the composite after intermediate labels
are ignored, whereas Theorem 3.1 retains the actual labelled operations and
their full joint output. These are different preservation obligations.

